The weakest element is still the Call Trace pane, centre column x≈922-1527, and the contradiction I named last round has not moved. Read the three source-less sentences as a set. Code names a missing thing — no source text — and the void beneath it follows: there is nothing to draw. Values names a missing thing — no variable names — and the void beneath it follows. Call Trace does the opposite. Its sentence opens by asserting that a thing is *present*: "Frames are recorded, and without a file map they carry no function names or source positions." Frames are recorded. Then it renders none of them. Roughly 512px of the ~590px pane body, about 87% of the largest pane on the page and the one occupying the optical centre, is white space that the sentence does not account for, because the sentence explains why the frames would be *unlabelled*, not why they are *unlisted*. A recorded frame that has lost its name has not lost its depth, its ordinal or its program counter, and this engine is stepping through 345 instructions with a live `128 / 345` position — it demonstrably knows where in the frame stack that position sits. `#0 pc 0x…` rows, indented by depth, with the current frame marked, would be honest, would be the desktop app's own vocabulary, and would let the reader see the shape of an execution whose text is missing. Instead the one pane that promises data is the one pane that delivers none, so the page's largest void is its least explained, and a reader is invited to conclude that the render failed rather than that the chain is thin. The Values pane teaches the correct lesson in two lines twelve pixels below it. Fix in the pane, not in the sentence: list the recorded frames by index and program counter, or — if the capture truly retained no stack — change the sentence to say frames were *not* retained, because as written it is a promise the pane does not keep.

